.TH REGEXP 2
.SH NAME
compile,
destroy,
match,
match_from \- regular expression matching
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libregex"
.PP
.nf
.B "compile :: proc(pattern: string, fold: bool, allocator: runtime.Allocator) -> (re: ^Regex, ok: bool)"
.B "destroy :: proc(re: ^Regex)"
.B "match :: proc(re: ^Regex, text: string) -> (start, end: int, ok: bool)"
.B "match_from :: proc(re: ^Regex, text: string, from: int) -> (start, end: int, ok: bool)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These procedures compile and run regular expressions in the dialect
.IR regexp (6)
describes.
.PP
.I Compile
parses
.I pattern
and answers a
.B Regex
compiled from it, allocated from
.IR allocator ,
and
.BR ok .
.B Ok
is false when
.I pattern
does not parse, and
.I re
is then nil.
.I Fold
makes the match case-insensitive: the pattern and the text are compared
with letters lowered.
.I Destroy
frees a
.B Regex
and everything
.I compile
allocated for it.
.PP
.I Match
finds the leftmost match of
.I re
in
.I text
and answers its bounds,
.I start
and
.IR end ,
as byte offsets, and
.BR ok .
When several matches begin at the same leftmost point, the longest is
the one answered.
.I Match_from
is
.I match
starting the search at
.I from
rather than at the beginning of
.IR text ,
for a caller that replaces matches one at a time and must not find the
one it just replaced again.
.PP
There are no captures.
A match answers only the bounds of the whole pattern, never those of a
parenthesized subexpression, because neither
.IR grep (1)
nor
.IR sed (1)
needs one yet.
.PP
A pattern is compiled to a small instruction list and run as a Thompson
simulation: a set of positions in the program advances one character at
a time, so a match costs time proportional to the length of the pattern
times the length of the text, and never the exponential time a
backtracking matcher can take.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libregex/regex.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grep (1),
.IR sed (1),
.IR regexp (6)
.SH BUGS
There are no captures, so there is no
.B \e1
in a replacement.
There is no
.I rregexec
equivalent for runes: a pattern and its text are both bytes.
